# Docs audit — concise, non-duplicative

When creating or editing Markdown under `docs/`, treat `MoneyBallDB_index.md` as the router. Workflows ask “what do I need?” → index → the owning doc.

## Ownership

| Concern | Owning doc |
| ------- | ---------- |
| Why / principles / rules | `MoneyBallDB_manifesto.md` |
| Which assets / indicators | `MoneyBallDB_universe.md` |
| Tables, keys, fields | `MoneyBallDB_schema.md` |
| Providers | `MoneyBallDB_sources.md` |
| Build / update / derive / validate | `MoneyBallDB_workflow.md` |
| Field formulas / definitions | `MoneyBallDB_definitions.md` |

## Rules

1. **One owner per fact.** Do not restate another doc’s content. Link to the owner instead.
2. **Index first.** New docs get a numbered entry + one or two sentence blurb in the index, and a row in the Routing table when useful.
3. **Duplicate only if it speeds work** — e.g. Observed vs Derived *field labels* in schema, a one-line operational constraint with a manifesto link. Never copy full rationales.
4. **Prefer pointers over essays.** Example: schema may say “calendar begins 1957 (see manifesto §…)” — not the three reasons why.
5. **Stay concise.** Cut sections that only rephrase tables or philosophy already owned elsewhere.
6. **Cross-link companions** at the top of each doc; keep the index Routing table accurate when ownership changes.

## Bad vs good

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD: universe repeats manifesto philosophy -->
MoneyBallDB should not invent unavailable history. Missing values are OK...

<!-- ✅ GOOD: universe owns scope; points elsewhere for rules -->
Expansion and historical-integrity rules: see manifesto.
```

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD: schema repeats 1957 rationale -->
1957 because SPY..., Saturdays..., S&P 500...

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
Calendar begins **1957** (rationale: manifesto). Other datasets need not.
```
